ratvar-rules = Ratvar’s Rows Rules:
    Players must first have a deck of five Ratvarian Trading Cards. It may have no more than one Ultrarare (UR) card, two Rare (R) cards, and any number of Common (C) cards. Players are encouraged to label their cards to claim ownership.

    Ratvar’s Rows is played on a 3x3 grid. The players alternate turns playing one card at a time from their hand (a coin flip, or tossing a card to see who the highest number faces, decides who goes first). Players can search their entire deck for which card they want each turn. They should both be in an agreed orientation, and Ratvarian boards have a designated “up”.

    Each card has four numbers, one for each direction (up, right, down, left, listed in that order in its name); if a placed card’s number is higher than the adjacent defending card of the opposing color, the defender is captured, flipping to the attacker's color. The cards' sides rank from 1-7, with A being Ace.

    Aces are special. Unless a special rule is agreed before the match, Aces count as an 8 on the turn they are placed, and as a 0 at all other times.
    When all spaces are filled, the player with the most cards of their color wins!

    Decks are limited to 1 UR, 2 R, and any number of C.

    Alternative Rules:
    Optional rules. Most tournaments run Open Combo as the ruleset. 

    OPEN: Players play with their decks visible on the table. Common rule.
    COMBO: Flipped cards count as though they were newly placed by the active player (reactivating Aces). A card can only be flipped once per turn.
    EIGHTS: Aces always count as an 8.
    MATCH: Matching a newly-placed number to an existing opponent’s card captures it.
    DRAW: Decks are kept blind and shuffled in their boxes. One card is drawn each turn.
    ANTE: Players must add an agreed amount to a prize pool to play. Winner immediately claims the pot on victory.
    KEEPS: High stakes. Players select one of their opponents cards (generally written down and signed beside the game). At the end of best of five, the loser surrenders their chosen card to the winner.
